 the past we've been working  with our overseas suppliers to get costs  down shoppers need to be ready for  whatever is not out there so maybe try  sending santa a second or third gift  idea in case the supply chain backlog  hits his workshop too  jennifer johnson global news washington  from kandahar to canada next an afghan  refugee begins a new chapter with new  challenges  tensions between ukraine and russia have  taken a dramatic turn with allegations  of a coming coup  the ukrainian president claims his  officials have uncovered a plot by a  group of russians to overthrow his  government as early as next week he says  ukrainian intelligence obtained audio  recordings that suggest one of ukraine's  wealthiest businessmen is also involved  in recent weeks ukraine and western  allies have raised alarm over russia's  military buildup near its border with  ukraine both the kremlin and the  ukrainian oligarch have denied any  involvement  well for weeks now we have heard  heart-wrenching reports from inside  afghanistan but there are also stories  of hope last month we first introduced  you to muhammad ishmael also known as  captain smiley his family was hiding in  a safe house terrified the taliban would  find them ishmael spent years protecting  canadian soldiers and now those veterans  have returned the favor they helped  ishmael escape kandahar and start a new  life in canada  muhammad ishmael's family just arrived  in toronto enjoying some fresh air and  breathing easier after escaping  afghanistan  for a long time i was afraid the taliban  would come and kill my husband  for six years ishmael provided security  for the canadian military in kandahar  the soldiers nicknamed him captain  smiley why captain smiley captain smiley  like all the thai are smiling but his  was serious work so he was instrumental  in helping to keep everything secure  and safe around the base randy scott  worked alongside ishmael and the two  became close they saved our bacon on  more than one occasion  how are you we took ishmael and his son  for a tour of their new home city i see  city a tall building  before arriving in canada ishmael's  family had never left afghanistan never  seen a skyscraper  a street car or even a squirrel  it's it's good city good place  very clean green  and everything is awesome awesome and  overwhelming  ready  almost everything here is foreign  unfamiliar ishmael can't read english  and his family arrived with little money  yeah he's very different and that's been  hard  wahadat wish knows the feeling at that  time when i arrived there was no one to  help me the former military interpreter  moved to toronto with his family in  2011. for eight years he struggled to  find steady work i went to depression  but a couple of years ago wish got a  full-time job and his situation improved  thanks in part to support from a  familiar friend  how are you my friend  randy scott worked with both men in  kandahar he lives near vancouver and  within days of ishmael arriving in  toronto he connected him with wish who  offered to help i'm very happy captain  ismail is here with us that's me very  good for for me i come in very happy  like what what can i say  and there's services and there's some  funding available and things like that  but it takes  a community to help welcome them  and to help them to become established  and to get back up on their feet as it  were hockey puck  government data shows first generation  refugees typically struggle but their  children are much more likely to thrive  and for that reason ishmael says there's  no looking back  i try my best  how can i  do good thing in this country  and you can watch our full documentary  on captain smiley's fight to escape  afghanistan and start a new life in  canada that's tomorrow night at seven  o'clock on the new reality right here on  global  nova scotia's population calculation  next the small province gets set for a  major milestone  [Music]  nova scotia is on the brink of a  historic milestone it's just a few  hundred people away from marking a  population of one million analysts say  it will help the province market itself  and bolster its economy ross lord  reports on what's driving the population  boom  okay so now with your partner  long before she moved to halifax and  started taking english language lessons  former syrian refugee nora alto wheel  was intimidated by coming to canada five  years later she says her family loves it  here among the benefits free public  school for one of their sons who lives  with autism an option they wouldn't have  in war-torn syria i have all my  human rights here it's not  in canada they look  to you not  where are you from  it's they look you  the human accepting more syrian refugees  per capita than most canadian provinces  has helped nova scotia edge closer to a  coveted milestone a population of one  million nova scotia will join six other  provinces in eclipsing the one million  mark nowhere close to ontario at the  upper end and still some work to do to  reach saskatchewan at the lower side but  well past the territories and the other  three atlantic provinces and a million  sounds good doesn't it this marketing  analyst says it will also make nova  scotia more attractive to people from  other parts of canada who've already  been moving to the province in greater  frequency during the pandemic it's not  all upside it's going to put  absolutely to put some strain on health  care it's going to put some strain on  transportation but still worthwhile if  it makes up for the trend of more nova  scotians passing away than being born  and young people moving away to find  work nova scotia is unfortunately known  as an out  migration province but you know the the  immigration is certainly something that  we absolutely need the changing face of  nova scotia society is reflected in  growing niche sections of grocery stores  and in more citizenship ceremonies nora  herself plans to apply for canadian  citizenship soon after completing this  round of language lessons  ross lord global news halifax  1 million reasons to celebrate and that  is global national for this friday night  i'm jeff semple christmas is still a few  weeks away but that doesn't stop  everyone from decorating early so we  leave you tonight with the vibrant  lights of the bright knights display in  vancouver stanley park the holiday  staple was put on pause last year due to  the pandemic but starting tonight it is  welcoming visitors back and brightening  their day  thank you for watching robin gill will  be back at the anchor desk with you  tomorrow have a great night  [Music]  you
 

